[SYNTH_REPLY] Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD) is a gastrointestinal motility disorder characterized by reflux of stomach contents into the esophagus or oral cavity, with heartburn and regurgitation as its typical symptoms [18]. The condition affects quality of life and may lead to erosive esophagitis, esophageal strictures, and Barrett esophagus, a precursor to esophageal adenocarcinoma [18]. Symptoms potentially attributable to GERD are among those most commonly reported to primary care providers in the outpatient setting [20].

## Typical Symptoms

The hallmark symptoms of GERD are heartburn and regurgitation of gastric contents into the oropharynx [18]. In young children, clinical manifestations are varied and nonspecific, necessitating careful diagnostic evaluation [19]. In children, GER itself is often physiological and managed conservatively, whereas GERD is a less common pathologic process causing troublesome symptoms that may require medical management [19].

## Pathogenic Factors and Risk Factors

Pathogenic factors contributing to GERD include abnormal transient relaxations of the lower esophageal sphincter, defects in esophageal mucosal defense, impaired esophageal and gastric motility and clearance, and anatomical defects of the lower esophageal reflux barrier such as hiatal hernia [19]. Long-term use of proton-pump inhibitors, the most effective pharmacological treatment, is associated with bone fractures, chronic renal disease, acute renal disease, community-acquired pneumonia, and Clostridium difficile intestinal infection [18]. Clinical management approaches include proton pump inhibitor trials and specific indications warranting referral to gastroenterology specialists [20].

## Associated Clinical Trials and Publications

Several clinical trials address GERD treatment, including a study of famotidine in subjects with non-erosive GERD [4], a pantoprazole efficacy trial in patients older than 18 years with acid reflux symptoms [7], and a placebo-controlled study of endoscopic fundoplication for GERD treatment [16]. Additional trials include a natural history study of gastroesophageal reflux in children and adolescents [10] and an on-demand treatment of reflux disease trial [13]. Relevant publications include PMID 29132520 [5], PMID 33351044 [8], PMID 36170502 [11], PMID 37034973 [14], and PMID 37516506 [17]. The entity Gastroesophageal Reflux is recognized in the literature [3], and the MedGen concept is titled Gastroesophageal reflux (GERD) [2]. A pediatric review is titled Gastroesophageal reflux disease in children: What's new right now? [12]. Multiple PubMed records are titled Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease [1][6][9][15].
